{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '140,178p;195,245p'\"","aggregated_output":"   140\t### The pass\n   141\t\n   142\tA pass classifies nothing until a first tick has been observed. At each pass,\n   143\tin this order:\n   144\t\n   145\t1. **Drain every wired tick cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`. The newest\n   146\t   reading drained, or the newest previously observed if none were drained, is\n   147\t   **now** for the whole pass.\n   148\t2. **Drain every wired lease-fact cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`, stamping\n   149\t   each fact with that same *now*, and apply it: an eligible renewal advances\n   150\t   the lease's last relevant observation to *now* and returns it to `known`; a\n   151\t   valid `:released` from the holder marks it released; a `:dao.stream/gap`\n   152\t   marks every lease on that medium `unknown` with *now* as its resumed\n   153\t   reading. Each cursor's drain may be bounded by a composition-declared\n   154\t   budget; a cursor still yielding when the budget is spent has not completed\n   155\t   its observation, and while any wired fact cursor is so truncated, the pass\n   156\t   classifies no `:silence` for the leases that truncated medium carries —\n   157\t   `:release`, `:cap`, `:policy` and pending retries are never suppressed. An\n   158\t   incomplete drain may delay a reclaim; it may never manufacture one.\n   159\t3. **Answer and grant.** Proposals drained this pass may be answered, and\n   160\t   unsolicited grants authored, both seeded and stamped with this pass's *now*.\n   161\t   A grant authored between passes is seeded with the next pass's *now*.\n   162\t4. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds, and the\n   163\t   first that holds in this order is its cause: it is already `pending`, and\n   164\t   keeps the cause it carries; a valid `:released` from its holder has been\n   165\t   observed, `:release`; its tenure start is further back than\n   166\t   `:dao.lease/max`, `:cap`; the grantor's own policy ends it, `:policy`; its\n   167\t   interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus\n   168\t   tolerance, `:silence`. A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for\n   169\t   `:silence` only once, in addition, a full duration has passed since its\n   170\t   resumed reading.\n   171\t5. **Reclaim** each due lease by its composition-supplied procedure, marking it\n   172\t   `pending` with its cause first. The procedure is idempotent and reports\n   173\t   whether it succeeded; a reclaim that fails or does not report leaves the\n   174\t   lease `pending` and unrecorded.\n   175\t6. **Record** `:lapsed`, carrying the cause the ledger holds, for each lease\n   176\t   whose reclaim succeeded. On `:dao.stream/ok` the lease leaves the ledger;\n   177\t   otherwise it stays `pending` and the next pass reaches it again through the\n   178\t   first clause of step 4.\n   195\t### Restart\n   196\t\n   197\tA grantor that has lost its ledger **reclaims and re-grants**: for each\n   198\tresource it still possesses it performs the reclaim first, then grants afresh.\n   199\tThe reclaim ends the prior tenure; a new grant alone does not.\n   200\t\n   201\tThis requires an inventory of possessed resources and a recoverable holder for\n   202\teach, both of which belong to the resource rather than to this vocabulary.\n   203\tA grantor with neither persists its ledger instead.\n   204\t\n   205\t## The holder\n   206\t\n   207\t- **Observe the grant before acting**, and establish that the grantor authored\n   208\t  it by the same attribution its composition supplies to the judge. A holder\n   209\t  that has not observed its grant holds nothing.\n   210\t- **Renew at less than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own\n   211\t  tick stream. A renewal is an append returning `:dao.stream/ok`; any other\n   212\t  outcome does not advance the holder's bound.\n   213\t- **Stop acting at the bound** — the earlier of the duration since the later of\n   214\t  its last renewal and its observed grant, and the cap the grant carries —\n   215\t  whether or not anything has been heard.\n   216\t- **Release when done.** The grantor still performs the reclaim and records\n   217\t  it.\n   218\t- **Treat an unusable reading as unanswerable.** A reading that is not a\n   219\t  single-entry `{unit, positive integer magnitude}` within the composition's\n   220\t  table answers nothing: every holder discipline fails closed on it, and a\n   221\t  flow that guards on its bound alone does not act.\n   222\t\n   223\tFor cap purposes, the holder measures the cap interval from the reading at\n   224\twhich it observed the grant; that reading may be later than the grantor\n   225\tjudge's tenure start. The holder's observation-flight interval does not\n   226\textend, delay, or alter the judge's cap or reclaim decision; a resource\n   227\trequiring exclusion must enforce it through fencing.\n   228\t\n   229\tThe bound bounds attention, not access; a holder needing exclusion obtains it\n   230\tfrom the resource.\n   231\t\n   232\t## Composition duties\n   233\t\n   234\tA composition that grants leases owes all of the following.\n   235\t\n   236\t- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime driving it at a declared cadence** —\n   237\t  the maximum interval between *completed* passes.\n   238\t- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for every holder.\n   239\t- **A tolerance** supplied to the judge, possibly zero.\n   240\t- **An attribution resolver** for the judge and for every holder observing a\n   241\t  grant — its own answer to who authored a fact, by per-author media, an\n   242\t  envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity, since DaoStream supplies\n   243\t  none. One handed no resolver, or one incompatible with its medium, is refused\n   244\t  at assembly.\n   245\t- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '530,575p;667,720p'\"","aggregated_output":"   530\t## 5. Conventions that are not protocol\n   531\t\n   532\tNone of these has a frame. Each is peers using sections 2 and 3.\n   533\t\n   534\t**The toy.** Peer B holds a handle `s` over the single value `\"hello\"`,\n   535\tidentity `\"str-1\"`, complete history, surface `#{:reader}`. B's table is\n   536\t`{\"str-1\" {:handle s :surface #{:reader}}}`. B accepts WebSocket connections\n   537\tas `dao.stream.ws.md` specifies and drives, per accepted connection,\n   538\t`ws-project` and then `mirror-step` over the projected reader and the\n   539\tsocket handle. B publishes `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote\n   540\t:dao.stream/identity \"str-1\" :dao.stream/channel <B's ws descriptor>}`.\n   541\tPeer A calls `attach!`; the link dials and sends the `descriptor` probe;\n   542\tthe answer carries `:dao.stream.remote/surface #{:reader}`. A calls\n   543\t`cursor` with `:oldest`: no answer filed, the link sends the request and\n   544\tanswers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true`; on the next call\n   545\tthe drain files the source's `ok` with `c0`. `next c0` proceeds the same\n   546\tway: `blocked`, then `ok \"hello\" c1`, and `next c1` is `end`. Nothing knew\n   547\tit was a string.\n   548\t\n   549\t**Request and response service.** A peer S enters `requests` with surface\n   550\t`#{:writer}` and `answers` with surface `#{:reader}` in its table and runs\n   551\tan interpreter over the local ends; a peer P attaches reflections to both,\n   552\tmints `:newest` on `answers` first, appends `{:svc/request r ...}` through\n   553\t`requests`, and reads `answers` until it observes `:svc/request r`. Request\n   554\tids are self-minted random values so callers sharing one `answers` stream\n   555\tdo not collide; a caller that needs private answers asks S for a pair. S is\n   556\ta \"server\" only in that its table has those entries and its interpreter has\n   557\tthat policy, and either peer may serve such a pair to the other over the\n   558\tsame channel. This is Linda's request medium, and the shape `yin.repl` and\n   559\tcontent lookup take (implementation plan, section 1). The meeting board and\n   560\tthe relay pair are section 4's instances of it.\n   561\t\n   562\t**Stream names.** A name is lookup data and never a `:dao.stream/identity`:\n   563\tno descriptor, cursor or answer carries a name under that key. A stream\n   564\twhose identity is new each time its holder starts is reached by a name in\n   565\tits peer's name map, resolved before each attachment and again after the\n   566\tchannel is lost. **The head board** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md` 5.1) is\n   567\tthis convention: the publisher's table holds its board ring under the\n   568\tring's own identity with surface `#{:reader}`, its name map holds\n   569\t`\"yin.head/\"` followed by the principal, mapped to that identity, and a\n   570\treader resolves the name and attaches the descriptor answered.\n   571\t\n   572\t**Peer names.** A name is a self-certifying hash of a public key, the\n   573\tkickoff-hash form of `dao.stream.discovery.md`. It appears only inside\n   574\tconvention payloads. Nothing authenticates it until authentication exists.\n   575\t\n   667\t## 8. Unification and contract boundary\n   668\t\n   669\t`dao.jing.content` succeeds `dao.jing.remote` whole. The implementation\n   670\tplan lists the successor vocabulary (section 1), the network-path fates\n   671\t(section 5) and the five `dao.stream.md` amendments (section 2), which add\n   672\tno network or capability concept.\n   673\t\n   674\t[impl-plan]: ./dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
