{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '696,750p;854,880p'\"","aggregated_output":"   696\t## Retention and Gaps\n   697\t\n   698\tRetention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the\n   699\tstream or writer surface's declared nature; a locally created stream records\n   700\tthem through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\n   701\tthe oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send\n   702\tbuffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\n   703\t`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\n   704\tpermanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the\n   705\toutcomes and standardizes no policy.\n   706\t\n   707\t**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\n   708\tpolicy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\n   709\tpositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\n   710\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n   711\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n   712\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n   713\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\n   714\treceives a `gap`.\n   715\t\n   716\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   717\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   718\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   719\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   720\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n   721\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n   722\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n   723\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n   724\t\n   725\t### Complete history\n   726\t\n   727\tSome consumers require *complete* history rather than *retained* history: an\n   728\tinterpreter that derives state by replaying a log from its beginning is wrong,\n   729\tnot merely stale, if it replays a suffix. The contract already carries what\n   730\tsuch a consumer needs, in two mechanisms that answer different questions.\n   731\t\n   732\t**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\n   733\tdeclaration in its existing form — the outcome's precondition is impossible by\n   734\tthe transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\n   735\t(see *Surfaces*). Retention is declared through the transport's nature and, for\n   736\ta locally created stream, through its creation specification; it is\n   737\tconfiguration provenance. There is no way to ask a handle about it and none is\n   738\tadded. A consumer requiring complete history therefore requires *a transport of\n   739\tthat declared nature*, and the composition that wires it is what supplies one.\n   740\t\n   741\t**A kept origin cursor gives detection.** On a transport that can evict, an\n   742\torigin cursor turns a loss that would otherwise be invisible into a reported\n   743\t`gap`, and the consumer aborts or recovers on its own policy. It does not make\n   744\tthe history complete; it makes incompleteness observable.\n   745\t\n   746\tBoth stand, and they are not substitutes. Where completeness is a correctness\n   747\trequirement, only the declared transport delivers it, and a kept origin cursor\n   748\tis the honest failure mode that remains when completeness was not wired. Where\n   749\ta consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\n   750\tcursor is right and sufficient.\n   854\t### OD-4. A logical stream can close but cannot end\n   855\t\n   856\t**The assumption.** *Complete history* obliges a transport to retain every\n   857\telement \"for as long as the logical stream exists\", and *Close* says close\n   858\t\"erases nothing\". Nothing says how a logical stream stops existing. The\n   859\tcontract assumes storage is either bounded by eviction or kept forever.\n   860\t\n   861\t**Evidence.** There is no destroy, truncate, compact, or redact anywhere in the\n   862\tsurface or in any transport. `memory-log` ends only when its process does. What\n   863\t`next`, `cursor`, or `attach!` answer for a stream whose medium was removed out\n   864\tof band is undefined; the nearest outcomes are `transport-error` and\n   865\t`not-found`, and the document chooses neither.\n   866\t\n   867\t**What breaks.** Reclaiming disk under a durable log; snapshot-and-truncate\n   868\tcompaction, the ordinary way a replayed log stays bounded; erasure that law or\n   869\ta user requires. All three are forbidden to a transport that declared complete\n   870\thistory, and consumers are wired to that declaration as a correctness\n   871\trequirement.\n   872\t\n   873\t**Proposed resolution.**\n   874\t\n   875\t1. Name a third retention nature beside *evicting* and *complete*:\n   876\t   **compacted**. A compacted transport may drop a prefix it has summarized. A\n   877\t   cursor into the dropped prefix receives `gap` exactly as under eviction, and\n   878\t   the result map may carry, under a transport-owned key, a descriptor or\n   879\t   content address for the summary that replaces the prefix. A consumer that\n   880\t   requires complete history is not wired to one; a consumer that can resume\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
