{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -A35 -B5 'dao.jing.content|:dao.jing/' docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md | head -120; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '319,343p'; nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '89,110p'\"","aggregated_output":"4-[`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md); this file carries the\n5-migration design, the completion criteria and slices, and the companion-edit\n6-inventory moved out of that spec to keep it short. Subordinate to the spec\n7-on any disagreement.\n8-\n9:## 1. What replaces `dao.jing.remote`: `dao.jing.content`\n10-\n11-Content lookup is a request and response service over remote streams\n12:(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 5), in one module family, `dao.jing.content`:\n13:the namespaces `dao.jing.content` (`serve-step`), `dao.jing.content.step`\n14:(`step`), `dao.jing.content.driver` (`driver`, clj only) and\n15:`dao.jing.content.async` (`async`).\n16-\n17-- **Vocabulary**, a payload convention: `{:jing/request r :jing/get\n18-  address}` answered by `{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64}`;\n19-  `{:jing/request r :jing/put address :jing/bytes b64}` answered by\n20-  `{:jing/request r :jing/result :inserted | :present}`. `r` is a\n21-  self-minted random value. Bytes travel as padded standard Base64 text in\n22-  the application value; message boundaries are the channel codec's, one\n23-  application value per message, so the vocabulary rides Transit-JSON text\n24-  or canonical CBOR alike (`dao.jing.cbor.md`, Remote and DHT).\n25:- **The interpreter** (`dao.jing.content/serve-step`): a pure step over a\n26-  local `requests` reader and `answers` writer against a `dao.jing` handle.\n27-  It runs the one ingress canonicality check on every put.\n28-- **The ingress check**, one shared function `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`,\n29-  moved from today's private `dao.jing.remote/accept-bytes!` and used by\n30-  the interpreter, by the stepped client on every `get` answer and\n31-  `:present` verify, and by `yin.vm.linker`. The three copies become one\n32-  (today: `dao/jing/remote.cljc`, `dao/jing/remote/step.cljc`, and the\n33-  linker's own; only the first includes the canonical CBOR decode).\n34:- **The stepped client** (`dao.jing.content.step`): the `request-put`,\n35-  `request-get`, `request-materialize`, `step`, `abandon` shape of\n36-  `dao.jing.remote.step`, over a `requests` writer and an `answers` reader\n37-  that are reflections or local handles. This is the portable interface,\n38-  per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n39:- **The blocking driver** (`dao.jing.content.driver`, clj only): host\n40-  policy that steps the client and sleeps, returning a synchronous\n41-  `dao.jing` handle. It replaces `connect-content!`; its consumers are the\n42-  coordinate opener and, through it, `dao.space` (the published-index\n43-  coordinate opens it at `src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc`) and `yin.repl.link`\n44-  on the JVM. `yin.vm.content` has no remote path today (its handle is an\n45-  argument), so it gains one only by being handed a coordinate-built handle.\n46:- **The async facade** (`dao.jing.content.async`): replaces\n47-  `dao.jing.remote.async` over the new stepped client, same consumer API;\n48-  `dao.data.btree.storage` is the consumer (`hydrate-async`,\n49-  `store-tree-async`).\n50:- **The coordinate**: `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests\n51:  <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` on every\n52-  host, resolved by `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` to the driver on clj and to\n53-  the stepped client elsewhere. The `:url` form is gone; its consumers are\n54-  the tests and the coordinate itself.\n55-\n56-## 2. Contract boundary\n57-\n58-The `dao.stream.md` changes are the only changes to that contract: OD-1,\n59-OD-2 and OD-3; the `:dao.stream/refused` rows on `cursor`, `next` and\n60-`append!`; and the composed-handle sentence in Surfaces. They add no network\n61-or capability concept. Section 5 holds the fate of every network path;\n62-section 4 lists companion-document edits.\n63-\n64-## 3. Completion criteria and implementation slices\n65-\n66-Ordered. Each names files, host lanes and its proof. Verification commands\n67-are in `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`.\n68-\n69-+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n70-| #  | Slice and files                                            | Proves                                                       | Lanes         |\n71-+====+============================================================+==============================================================+===============+\n72-| 0  | Contract and source alignment: the `dao.stream.md`         | Existing consumers pass; `dao.stream.observe/step` and the   | clj cljs cljd |\n73-|    | amendments of the plan's section 2 are accepted;           | engine treat an unrecognized outcome as refused, not a       |               |\n74-|    | `:dao.stream/refused` added to the closed outcome sets     | throw; the outcome sets admit the refusal.                   |               |\n75-|    | for `cursor`, `next` and `append!` in                      |                                                              |               |\n76-|    | `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`; `dao.stream.observe`,          |                                                              |               |\n77-|    | `yin.vm.engine` follow the unrecognized-outcome rule.      |                                                              |               |\n78-+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n79-| 1  | `dao.stream.middleware`: `apply-request`, `wrap`, `gate`,  | Position rule under a value cipher over a ring buffer; a     | clj cljs cljd |\n80-|    | `present`, a metering exemplar, an index-interpreter        | gate with the channel allow-list refuses with                |               |\n81-|    | exemplar publishing decisions. `src/cljc/dao/stream/        | `:dao.stream/refused`; a gate reads the latest decision      |               |\n82-|    | middleware.cljc`, tests.                                   | through a capacity-1 medium after eviction; a filter cannot  |               |\n83-|    |                                                            | be expressed.                                                |               |\n84-+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n85-| 2  | `dao.stream.remote` core: `mirror-step`, link, reflection, | The toy over two in-process ring buffers as the channel;     | clj cljs cljd |\n86-|    | remote descriptor dispatch, the protocol errors.           | `gap` from an evicting source crosses verbatim with the      |               |\n--\n92-| 3  | WebSocket channel composition: `ws-project` at each end;   | The toy across a socket on clj to Node, Node to clj, cljd    | clj cljs cljd |\n93-|    | mirror steps over accepted and dialed attachments;         | to clj; both directions serve on one channel; browser        |               |\n94-|    | retire `:ws/accept`, disclaim and the served-path table    | dials and reads; a closed connection is observed as the      |               |\n95-|    | in `dao.stream.ws`.                                        | link's `end`.                                                |               |\n96-+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n97:| 4  | Content service: the `dao.jing.content` family (section 1) | `dao.jing.remote` tests ported and passing over the new      | clj cljs cljd |\n98-|    | with `serve-step`, `step`, `driver` (clj), `async`;         | module; `dao.space.index`, btree hydration, the linker M3    |               |\n99-|    | `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`; `dao.jing.coordinate` new         | and M4 tests unchanged in outcome; no require of             |               |\n100-|    | coordinate; delete `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc`,        | `dao.jing.remote` remains.                                   |               |\n101-|    | `remote/step.cljc`, `remote/async.cljc` and their tests.   |                                                              |               |\n102-+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n103-| 5  | REPL as a service: `yin.repl.serve` and `connect` over     | `(connect \"daostream:ws://...\")` resolves to a remote        | clj cljs cljd |\n104-|    | `requests` and `answers` reflections; delete               | descriptor pair; the demo REPLs work; `yin.repl.link` serves |               |\n105-|    | `dao.stream.serving`, `dao.stream.rpc.ws`, the apply wire  | content through slice 4; `dao.stream.rpc` is reworked over   |               |\n106-|    | envelope from `dao.stream.apply`; rework or retire         | reflections or retired in the same slice, because the apply  |               |\n107-|    | `dao.stream.rpc`.                                          | envelope it requires is deleted here.                        |               |\n108-+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n109-| 6  | UDP channel: `dao.stream.udp` with fragmentation keyed by  | The toy over UDP with a 48 KiB value (inside the default     | clj cljs cljd |\n110-|    | attachment, address, direction and id; host seams          | 64 KiB maximum); loss injection recovers by resend;          |               |\n111-|    | `dao.stream.datagram.jvm`, `.node`, and `.dart`.           | oversize beyond the maximum answers `transport-error`        |               |\n112-|    |                                                            | with reason `oversize`.                                      |               |\n113-+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n114-| 7  | Pair channel and the meeting and relay conventions with    | Two peers behind a simulated restricted NAT punch; behind    | clj           |\n115-|    | leases: `dao.stream.remote.pair`, `dao.stream.remote.meet`;| a simulated symmetric NAT they relay; a pair whose holder    |               |\n116-|    | a meeting gate refuses past the pair bound.                | stops renewing answers `not-found` after duration plus       |               |\n117-|    |                                                            | tolerance; a reconnect within it finds the pair intact; a    |               |\n118-|    |                                                            | meeting peer past its bound refuses, not silences.          |               |\n119-+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n120-| 8  | UCF facade: `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`      | The acceptance row: a string-backed stream migrates with a   | clj cljs cljd |\n121-|    | 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 lift and lower through remote descriptors  | kept cursor and resumes; forced eviction yields `gap` with   |               |\n122-|    | with the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` cursor profile.           | the source's cursor.                                         |               |\n123-+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n124-\n125-Slice 7 also proves the pair rule of the spec's 3.3: a `gap` on a pair's\n126-`in` ends that link's channel, outstanding appends are reported\n127-`append-unknown`, and `attach!` on the same pair descriptor resumes.\n   319\t## Reads\n   320\t\n   321\tThe storage read operation resolves a strict content address to the exact\n   322\topaque value stored at that address. `dao.jing/get` accepts only\n   323\tregistered `:segment/<algorithm>-<digest>` addresses; arbitrary keys and mutable\n   324\troots are outside DaoJing and throw before a backend is consulted.\n   325\t\n   326\tA reader may access the target locally or through a remote transport\n   327\t(`dao.jing.content` over `dao.stream.remote.md`, which deprecates\n   328\t`dao.jing.remote`; `dao.jing.dht`). Location changes how bytes are obtained,\n   329\tnot how their identity or meaning is determined.\n   330\t\n   331\tHigher layers expose the semantic compositions the stream participates in. A\n   332\tquery reads a published index descriptor containing a serializable\n   333\tcontent-store coordinate plus an immutable manifest address.\n   334\t`dao.jing.coordinate/open!` interprets coordinates such as\n   335\t`{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/file :path ...}` or\n   336\t`{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests ... :dao.jing/answers ...}`\n   337\t(two remote descriptors, `dao.stream.remote.md` section 8; today only the\n   338\tdeprecated JVM form `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :url ...}` exists)\n   339\tinto local handles;\n   340\tunsupported coordinates fail closed. These coordinates are caller-supplied\n   341\tvalues, not state inferred from storage. DaoJing does not infer a source or\n   342\tcoordinate from the stream that carried a payload, and no content address is\n   343\tever treated as a mutable root.\n    89\twhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\n    90\tthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\n    91\tto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\n    92\tdeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\n    93\tmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\n    94\tnot an upward callback.\n    95\t\n    96\tHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\n    97\tplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\n    98\ttransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\n    99\tadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\n   100\tevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\n   101\tfor facts it owns.\n   102\t\n   103\tWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\n   104\tadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\n   105\ttransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\n   106\tthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\n   107\tconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n   108\t\n   109\tA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\n   110\tnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"The `/s/` versus `/k/` split fixes subscription consumption, and all six HTTP example forms now parse. The Architect’s rebuttal on performed writes versus delivery is sound. Remaining gaps include how the router learns tokens, how an ingress gap reaches session drivers, and how the browser locates the content service. The proposed compaction option also conflicts with the current stream contract."}}
