{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'token|renewal|session-facts|gap|compaction|open-exchanges' docs/design/dao.star.md docs/design/dao.stream.http.md\"","aggregated_output":"docs/design/dao.stream.http.md:34:`egress-step`, `open-exchanges`, answering the open ids, together with the\ndocs/design/dao.stream.http.md:81:Eviction is loss. A reader whose cursor spans an ingress `gap` has missed\ndocs/design/dao.stream.http.md:128:- an egress `gap` closes every open exchange, since the step cannot say\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:30:1. **Events In**: A user clicks a button (`data-on-click=\"@post('/k/<token>')\"`). Datastar fires a standard HTTP request. The generic HTTP boundary (`dao.stream.http.md`) mints an exchange id, deposits the request as an unjudged `:http/request` event into the ingress `dao.stream` the composition wired, and holds the socket open. It reads nothing inside the request.\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:31:2. **Routing**: A router step in the `dao.star` composition reads the ingress with its own cursor, classifies each request by its path (*Sessions and request kinds*), and forwards it to the medium of the session its token names. The boundary does not route.\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:39:A **lineage** is one session's chain of occurrences: a root occurrence and every successor consumed input produced. Its identity is the root occurrence's id. Each occurrence has a **token**, unguessable, and every projected UI carries the token of the occurrence that rendered it. A token therefore names an occurrence, and through it a lineage; the lineage's **current** occurrence is the one with no successor. The router derives it from successor facts, never from a table it keeps.\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:41:The router classifies by path; the boundary reads neither path nor token.\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:45:| `/` (no token) | begin | The root program starts a fresh lineage and answers a complete `text/html` document: `datastar.js`, actions carrying the root token, and `data-init=\"@get('/s/<token>')\"`. |\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:46:| `/s/<token>` | subscribe | Opens the session's long-lived SSE exchange. Consumes nothing and advances nothing: the driver answers the head and a projection of the current occurrence, then every later patch goes here too. Reconnecting with the original URL is a new subscribe, resolved to the current occurrence however many inputs have passed. |\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:47:| `/k/<token>`, token current | input | Consumed by the current occurrence (*Inputs, order and replay*). |\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:48:| `/k/<token>`, token consumed | stale | Runs nothing. Answered by a projection of the current occurrence, then `:http/end`. |\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:49:| any token of an ended or unknown lineage | begin | Reclaimed, halted, or lost to a restart: answered as `/`. |\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:53:**Lineage facts.** The driver appends one fact per transition to the session-facts medium: `{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/occurrence O' :dao.star/predecessor O}` on consumption, `{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/ended :halted}` on halt, and the retirement fact under *Liveness* on reclaim. The router materializes token → lineage → current from these facts as `dao.space.index` materializes a covered index. The session-facts medium is wired with complete retention for the life of its lineages, or compaction that keeps each live lineage's chain; an evicting medium there is a host assembly defect.\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:65:**The token is the gate.** It is the request's only routing and idempotence key, and until ShiBi is load-bearing it is the sole gate on Mode 1 input: a pre-shared value in `dao.shibi.md`'s sense (owner ruling 2026-10-07).\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:69:**One input, one successor.** Consuming an input advances occurrence `O` to its successor `O'` and appends the lineage fact. The reply goes to the exchange that carried the consumed input; patches for the session go to its open subscription exchanges, which the driver holds as its own interpreter state, built from the subscribe requests and terminal events it has observed (*Liveness* says what an ingress gap does to that state).\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:81:**The open exchange is leased.** The judge runs inside the peer that holds the HTTP boundary and is composed with the boundary's `close-exchange` as reclaim procedure and `open-exchanges` as its inventory (`dao.stream.http.md`, Inventory). For each `:http/request` it observes it grants unsolicited on a lease medium it writes: subject `{:http/exchange x}`, holder the egress medium's identity, a duration in `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`. Reclaim closes the exchange; the adapter deposits `:http/closed`, then the judge records `:lapsed`. A responder that keeps an exchange open observes its grant and appends `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal :dao.lease/lease L}` to the egress medium at under half the duration, from its own control flow, against its own tick stream. The judge and `egress-step` are peer observers of that medium; each ignores the other's facts. A one-shot responder answers inside the granted duration and renews nothing. An exchange nobody answers lapses by `:silence`; Datastar's reconnect opens a new one.\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:83:**An ingress gap is channel loss**, on both sides of it. The judge, having missed request events, runs `dao.lease.md`'s restart rule over the inventory: every open exchange without a lease is granted afresh, so no exchange is held unleased. A driver whose cursor spans the gap cannot know which of its subscriptions survived; it forgets them all and renews none, they lapse by `:silence` and close, and Datastar's reconnect subscribes again. Nothing is reconstructed from the evicted window; the ingress window is sized against the router's and judge's lag, as `dao.lease.md` Sizing requires of any medium a judge reads.\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:85:**Keep-alive is the responder's.** On its tick it may append an `:http/chunk` carrying an SSE comment line. That chunk is bytes for the wire, so the host discovers a dead peer by writing; the renewal is evidence for the judge. Neither stands in for the other. A peer's close or a failed write is deposited as `:http/closed`; the responder reads it as data and drops the exchange, and the judge ends the exchange lease with cause `:policy`.\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:87:**The parked continuation is leased** (owner ruling 2026-10-07; the holder choice below is the architect's). A lineage nobody returns to is reclaimed through `dao.lease.md`, not by a timer. The peer holding the parked record is the grantor. When a lineage begins, its judge grants unsolicited, subject `{:dao.star/lineage S}`, holder `{:dao.star/lineage S}`, duration the composition's idle allowance, `:dao.lease/max` optional. Attribution is by envelope key: the session-facts medium is written only by session drivers, each stamping `:dao.star/lineage`, and the judge's resolver reads that key; until ShiBi, any writer of that medium can renew, the same exposure the exchange lease has. The driver observes its grant on the lease medium before relying on it, and renews from its own control flow at under half the duration: on each consumed input, and on its tick while it holds an open subscription. So a connected session is retained for as long as it stays connected, by design, bounded only by the cap; a disconnected one lapses after the idle allowance. Consumption does not release: the lease outlives every occurrence of the lineage and ends with the lineage, released by the driver on halt or lapsed on silence. Reclaim retires the runnable resource, the current parked record, and appends `{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/ended :lapsed}`; the lineage's history, its successor facts and its lease records are kept (`dao.lease.md`: a reclaim frees the resource, never the record). Every token of the lineage then resolves to begin. The subject is distinct from Mode 2's custody subject `{:yin.k/occurrence O}` on the arbitration medium. Leases are process-scoped until the composition settles the three durable duties `dao.lease.md` names.\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:89:Sizing: duration exceeds twice the renewal interval; the keep-alive interval stays under the shortest proxy or NAT idle timeout on the path; the ingress window exceeds router and judge lag; a boundary restart loses sockets and ledger together, so its exchange inventory to reclaim is empty. A false lapse is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:111:The path, all of it existing design: before the notice is appended, the lift publishes the body to the server's content store and the store's request/answer service is an entry in the server's table (`dao.stream.remote.md` section 5, the shape `dao.jing.content` takes per section 8), gated like any entry, with the lineage token as the pre-ShiBi credential. The browser's fetch is the stepped content client, clock-free; it verifies the body by re-hashing (UCF §7.8, lower step 2). Absence or refusal arrives as data, `not-found` or `refused`, and the browser then holds nothing; the exporter, a candidate like any other, proposes for itself under its own policy (UCF §7.7.4), so an unfetched offer strands nothing. Moving the body off the egress removes its disclosure to egress observers; confidentiality beyond that is the gate's, since an address is a name, not an authorization.\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:141:- **Multiplexed Correlation**: Connections are not mapped 1:1 to streams. The HTTP boundary observes a shared egress stream and routes by exchange id. The cost is shared: an egress `gap` closes every open exchange, and a composition under that pressure shards the egress.\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:145:- **No hidden state**: a driver's open subscriptions and the router's token index are interpreter state derived from media each reads with its own cursor, and both are rebuilt from those media or honestly lost on a gap.\ndocs/design/dao.star.md:149:1. **Thin Client**: A `yin.vm` agent receives a Datastar input through the HTTP boundary's ingress stream, answers with `:http/head`, SSE-formatted `:http/chunk`s and `:http/end` on the egress stream, parks, and resumes on the next input for its current token. Also: a request with no token begins a lineage; a subscribe consumes nothing and a re-subscribe after several inputs receives the current projection; a stale token is re-projected; a closed socket is observed as `:http/closed`; a silent exchange lapses and closes; a lineage nobody returns to lapses, its parked record is retired and its facts remain.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-07 06:24:01 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-07 13:24:01 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a11500-e74e-7aa2-a0d7-7a8a9561d334\n\n| Finding | Status | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Subscription consumes input | **Confirmed** | `dao.star.md:45–51` separates `/s/` subscriptions from `/k/` inputs and explicitly forbids stepping on subscription. |\n| 2. Consumed tokens lose lineage | **Still wrong** | The lineage lease fixes release-on-consumption (`:87`), but the facts at `:53` contain neither token bindings nor a root-creation fact. The claimed token index cannot yet be reconstructed. |\n| 3. Evicting ingress loses exchange state | **Still wrong** | Inventory and explicit subscription state are appropriate (`dao.stream.http.md:31–37`, `dao.star.md:69`). However, `dao.star.md:83` does not specify how a router-observed gap reaches drivers reading routed session media. |\n| 4. Reclaim deletes historical records | **Confirmed** | `dao.star.md:87` distinguishes retiring the runnable resource from preserving history and lease records. |\n| 5. Holder attribution unspecified | **Confirmed** | `dao.star.md:87` supplies holder identity, envelope attribution, grant observation, and the connected-but-idle policy. New lease-discipline issues remain below. |\n| 6. Q6 fetch path missing | **Still wrong** | `dao.star.md:111` names the content service, but the notice at `:109` still supplies no request/answer descriptors or content-store coordinate. Content absence is also misclassified. |\n| 7. Terminal/effect ordering | **Confirmed** | `dao.stream.http.md:62–68,99–112` removes cross-medium ordering and consistently ignores post-end effects. |\n| 8. Invalid EDN examples | **Confirmed** | Independently parsed both blocks with `clojure.edn/read`: three forms each, six total. |\n| 9. HTTP document names application technologies | **Confirmed** | `dao.stream.http.md:7–8` is application-agnostic; word-boundary search found no Datastar, SSE, or `yin.vm` mentions. |\n\nThe Architect’s partial rebuttal of finding 7 is sound: a performed write does not establish delivery. My original concern was the stronger claim that a terminal event identifies unperformed effects and precedes ignored effects across media. Those claims are now removed.\n\n| Severity | File:line | Invariant/evidence | Recommended correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **High** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:53` | Root creation emits no specified fact, and successor facts contain no token. Token → lineage → current therefore requires unstated information. | Define root and token-binding facts, or explicitly make occurrence identity the unguessable token. Require successful publication before exposing each token. |\n| **High** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:83` | The router reads ingress (`:31`); drivers read routed session media. An ingress gap need not produce a gap on any driver’s cursor, so drivers can keep renewing subscriptions whose terminal events were lost. | Define an explicit router loss event delivered to affected drivers, or give drivers their own ingress cursors. Specify terminal-event routing too. |\n| **High** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:53` | Chain-preserving compaction is offered as an operative alternative, but `dao.stream.md:861–870` says compaction is unavailable and forbidden for complete-history transports. OD-4’s resolution is proposed. | Require the existing complete-history transport now. Mark compaction conditional on a settled contract and snapshot/replay protocol. |\n| **Medium** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:87` | Session-facts is “written only by session drivers,” yet the possessing judge’s reclaim appends the retirement fact there. Author and authority are ambiguous. | Permit explicitly attributed judge-authored retirement facts, or specify stream-mediated delivery to the driver. Keep renewal attribution separate from retirement authority. |\n| **Medium** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:87` | Renewal on a consumed input does not address an input arriving after the holder’s bound but before judge reclaim. `dao.lease.md:213–215` requires the holder to stop regardless of notification. | Require a valid holder bound before consumption or projection that relies on tenure. Specify the outcome for late input; renewal must not silently revive expired holder authority. |\n| **Medium** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:73` | Opt-in forks create multiple successor branches, whereas `:39` defines current as “the one with no successor.” That ceases to be unique. | Define branch identity and subscription routing for opt-in forks, or explicitly defer fork operation pending those semantics. |\n| **Medium** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:109` | A channel descriptor does not identify the two streams required by the content client. The remote implementation plan §1 specifies `:dao.jing/requests` and `:dao.jing/answers`. | Include the content-store coordinate in the notice, or specify an explicit service-name discovery convention. |\n| **Medium** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:111` | Content absence is `:jing/found? false` under `dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:17–24`; remote `not-found` means a missing served stream. Shared answer streams also expose responses to their readers (`dao.stream.remote.md:549–555`). | Distinguish missing content, missing service, and refusal. Specify lineage-scoped address authorization and private or equivalently protected answers. |\n| **Low** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:83` | Granting previously unleased exchanges is inventory reconciliation, not `dao.lease.md`’s restart rule, which requires reclaim before re-grant (`:197–199`). | Name the reconciliation separately; reserve reclaim-and-regrant for actual lost-ledger recovery. |\n\n`open-exchanges` itself is sound: it exposes owned resource inventory to the locally composed judge without adding a DaoStream operation or privileged node.\n\nAll six owner decisions remain honored, and Q6 remains explicitly unconfirmed. **The documents are not yet ready for owner sign-off.** The remaining defects need architectural corrections within those decisions, especially token facts, gap propagation, and the unsupported compaction alternative. Files were not edited."}}
